{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7ef4109a52811dbd", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "Traditional vs. Roth 401(k) Accounts", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With a traditional 401(k), the tax benefit is upfront. With a Roth 401(k), the tax benefit comes later.", "question": null, "answer": "Traditional and Roth 401(k) accounts differ mainly in when taxes are paid. Traditional 401(k) contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, so taxes are paid when the savings are withdrawn. Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax dollars; account earnings and withdrawals are generally tax-free. A plan may let you allocate money between the two options, including contributing to both.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1183, "output_tokens": 215}}
{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:7ef4109a52811dbd", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "Traditional vs. Roth 401(k) Accounts", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With a traditional 401(k), the tax benefit is upfront. With a Roth 401(k), the tax benefit comes later.", "question": "How does the timing of tax benefits differ between traditional and Roth 401(k) accounts?", "answer": "A traditional 401(k) provides its tax benefit upfront: contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, with taxes paid when savings are withdrawn. A Roth 401(k) uses after-tax contributions, and account earnings and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1183, "output_tokens": 180}}
