{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:65fe4922da2f8097", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Investment Options", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "A 401(k) plan is an employer-sponsored retirement plan that gives employees a choice of investment options, often various mutual funds. A popular type of fund in 401(k) plans is called a target date fund.", "question": null, "answer": "A 401(k) plan lets employees choose how to invest their retirement savings. The available choices often include mutual funds, and target date funds are one popular type. These are the plan’s investment choices; the traditional and Roth 401(k) options describe different tax treatments for contributions and savings.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1180, "output_tokens": 491}}
